<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malik Maqbul Tilangani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1369">1369</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Mala Yugandharudu, Khan-i-Jahan Maqbul Tilangani, Jahan Khan, Gannama Nayaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malik Maqbul Tilangani was a commander of the Kakatiya Empire who, captured and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, rose to become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">Wazir</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> under Firuz Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a> (r. 1351–1388). As Gannama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> he commanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> Fort under Prataparudra; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-khan">Ulugh Khan</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a>, took Warangal in 1323, he was left in charge of the new province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> until rebels retook it and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. There he became finance minister and then Wazir, so trusted that Firuz Shah called him 'brother' and left the fiscal and general administration to him. He died in 1369, and his son Jauna Khan succeeded him as Wazir.</p></div>
